                    </item>
                    <item name="Description" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">A quick note</item>
                    <item name="Font" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Segoe UI</item>
                    <item name="InstanceGuid" type_name="gh_guid" type_code="9">6b883f77-7b66-493d-aa1d-fadfed2a8634</item>
                    <item name="Italic" type_name="gh_bool" type_code="1">false</item>
                    <item name="Name" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Scribble</item>
                    <item name="NickName" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Scribble</item>
                    <item name="Size" type_name="gh_single" type_code="5">20</item>
                    <item name="Text" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">For the assignment, follow the steps marked as -1-,-2-,-3-...

-1- Select the brick you want to plan for, using the step/back buttons in the counter.
-2- Redefine the pick plane if you like. Make sure the inverse kinematics works before
planning (to check set the robot visualisation to analytic inverse kinematics). This is 
your start configuration for motion planning.
-3- Make sure the robot visualisation is set to motion planning.
(Before step 4, it will show an error)
-4- Compute the motion plan. You can press this button again if you want to replan.
-5- Use the slider to visualize the robot and record images or videos.
-6- Save the motion plan to the assembly information model.

Extra -E-:
In order to access and visualise previous plans, you can switch the robot visualisation to
 ssembly, and toggle on show current part plan motions in assembly visualisation. Now 
you can use the slider -E- to visualize the robot and record images or videos.

Be creative and play around with the visualisation tools you have! If you want you can 
move the mobile base around, assemble multiple patches etc. It is all up to you!</item>
                  </items>
